  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure lifting through the region through\nthe morning will produce showers and thunderstorms with the \npotential for flash flooding across portions of the forecast \narea. \n\nThe low and associated warm front is slowly lifting through\nDelmarva overnight. As it does so, expect low level moisture\nadvection to continue. For areas south of the warm front\nalready, dewpoints were quick to increase into the lower to mid\n70s. Precipitable water values are still expected to increase\nabove 2 inches. Given the high precipitable water values and\nrelatively deep warm cloud layer, rain rates up to 2 inches per\nhour will be possible. \n\nWhile the heavy rain won't last long, this could be enough to\nlead to flash flooding particularly in urban areas. The primary\narea of concern is the I-95 corridor and surrounding urban \nareas and small streams where flash flooding is more possible \ngiven any rapid runoff. The Flood Watch for flash flooding in \nthese areas continues through Noon. \n\nThis system will be fairly progressive, lifting to Long Island \nby around midday Sunday. Precipitation tapers off late this\nmorning the low departs. A weak cold front swings through \nSunday afternoon behind the low followed by a secondary cold \nfront late tonight/early Monday Morning. Some light rain \nshowers will be possible with the secondary cold front, but with\ndry air already filtering in to the area by then, do not expect\nmuch more than light rain showers.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front Thursday into Friday may bring some \nshowers and thunderstorms. \n\nAn upper-level trough across central into eastern Canada is forecast \nto push a cold front into our area at the end of next week. While \nthe guidance, including ensembles, are in decent agreement showing \nthis trough, there are significant differences with the \namplification and therefore speed of the trough. This will have \nimpacts to the cold front itself. If the main energy slides far \nenough north of our area, the front may tend to be slower moving. \nThis scenario would open the door for a greater chance for a round \nof convection. A faster movement to the front would lessen the \nlength of convection over our area. The front may then stall to our \nsouth into next weekend, with one or more possible waves of low \npressure tracking along it with time. A plume of deeper moisture \nlooks to get drawn northward ahead of this front, therefore some \nheavy rain potential may exist with it and this also depends on \nwhether it stalls or not. Confidence remains low during this time \nframe.",
